/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Workflows_LegalReview License and Ownership Review
 * @ingroup Foundation_Workflows_ReleaseV1
 * @brief Interim restrictions and the evidence packet required for legal review.
 *
 * @warning This page is a project-management record, not legal advice. Only a
 * qualified legal professional reviewing the applicable facts and agreements
 * can approve the final license and distribution model.
 *
 * @section FoundationLegalReviewCurrent Current Position
 *
 * | Field | Current state |
 * | ----- | ------------- |
 * | Repository visibility | Public on GitHub |
 * | Source and documentation | Visible, but proprietary |
 * | External permission | None granted without a separate signed agreement |
 * | Published binaries | Five native Foundation 1.2.0 packages |
 * | Current notice | `LICENSE`: All Rights Reserved / No License Granted |
 * | Final license decision | `BLOCKED` pending qualified legal review |
 *
 * Public visibility, a GitHub release, a downloadable archive, generated
 * documentation, and a CMake package do not by themselves grant permission to
 * use Foundation. The current `LICENSE` applies to source, headers, examples,
 * documentation, build files, and binaries. GitHub-hosting rights that may be
 * required by GitHub's terms are not treated here as a general software
 * license.
 *
 * @section FoundationLegalReviewGuardrails Interim Guardrails
 *
 * Until the review is complete:
 *
 * - do not describe Foundation as open source, freeware, public domain, or
 *   generally available for use;
 * - do not grant an external-use exception in README, package metadata,
 *   release notes, email, or chat unless it is a deliberate written agreement;
 * - keep `LICENSE`, README, generated documentation, and release-package text
 *   consistent;
 * - do not accept third-party code contributions until ownership and
 *   contribution terms have been selected;
 * - keep third-party test/build dependencies out of Foundation release
 *   packages unless their notices and redistribution terms are reviewed;
 * - record every source or binary distribution already made, including its
 *   date, recipient, version, and accompanying terms.
 *
 * These guardrails reduce ambiguity; they do not cure an ownership or prior
 * licensing problem.
 *
 * @section FoundationLegalReviewFacts Repository Facts Collected
 *
 * The following facts are reproducible from the repository and should be
 * included in the review packet:
 *
 * - the Git history currently contains two author identities:
 *   `ChecheRomo96 <josemanuel.romo.96@gmail.com>` and
 *   `José Manuel Romo <79122732+ChecheRomo96@users.noreply.github.com>`;
 * - the repository notice names José Manuel Romo as the 2026 copyright holder;
 * - GoogleTest 1.18.0 is fetched from a pinned commit only when repository
 *   testing is enabled, and GoogleTest installation is disabled;
 * - release packages contain Foundation headers, the Release library, CMake
 *   package metadata, `LICENSE`, README, and CHANGELOG; examples are optional
 *   export artifacts rather than test dependencies;
 * - the published Foundation 1.2.0 release contains five native packages and
 *   their checksums;
 * - GitHub Actions uses external actions to check out source, transfer
 *   artifacts, and deploy documentation; those tools are workflow
 *   dependencies, not Foundation library contents.
 *
 * These observations are not a complete provenance audit. In particular, Git
 * authorship and a copyright notice do not independently prove ownership.
 *
 * @section FoundationLegalReviewCounsel Questions for Counsel
 *
 * Counsel should resolve and document at least the following:
 *
 * 1. **Ownership and employment obligations.** Confirm whether any invention
 *    assignment, employment, contractor, university, client, or confidentiality
 *    agreement could cover the project. Record when, where, on whose equipment,
 *    and within which role the relevant code was created.
 * 2. **Code provenance.** Identify code copied, adapted, generated, or
 *    reimplemented from older personal projects, employers, clients, books,
 *    forums, libraries, or generated output. Preserve the source and license of
 *    every permitted third-party portion.
 * 3. **Contributors.** Confirm whether anyone other than the named owner
 *    contributed code, design, documentation, examples, or assets and whether
 *    written assignments or contribution terms exist.
 * 4. **Prior grants and distributions.** Review private shares, published Git
 *    history, package downloads, forks, messages, and earlier license files for
 *    permissions that may already have been granted.
 * 5. **Dependency and notice obligations.** Review build, test, documentation,
 *    and CI dependencies and determine which notices, source offers, or other
 *    obligations apply to distributed artifacts or generated documentation.
 * 6. **Names and marks.** Confirm that `Foundation` and any future repository
 *    or product branding do not create an unacceptable trademark or
 *    source-identification risk.
 * 7. **Intended business model.** Choose whether Foundation will remain
 *    proprietary, become source-available, use a recognized open-source
 *    license, or use a dual/commercial model. Define permitted evaluation,
 *    internal, educational, embedded-product, and redistribution scenarios.
 * 8. **Warranty, liability, export, and jurisdiction terms.** Select provisions
 *    appropriate to the intended users and distribution countries rather than
 *    assuming the interim disclaimer is sufficient everywhere.
 *
 * @section FoundationLegalReviewPacket Evidence Packet Checklist
 *
 * Before the consultation, collect:
 *
 * - [ ] applicable employment, invention-assignment, contractor, NDA, and
 *       school agreements covering the development period;
 * - [ ] a dated development history identifying personal versus third-party
 *       equipment, accounts, time, and repositories;
 * - [ ] a list of imported or rewritten code and its original location;
 * - [ ] the complete contributor/author list and any written assignments;
 * - [ ] all historical `LICENSE`, README, package, and release texts;
 * - [ ] the dependency inventory with versions, purposes, licenses, and whether
 *       each dependency is shipped;
 * - [ ] a list of prior recipients, downloads, private distributions, and
 *       permissions communicated outside the repository;
 * - [ ] the desired use cases and restrictions for Foundation, downstream
 *       libraries, firmware products, precompiled libraries, and source
 *       distributions;
 * - [ ] the countries and entity/person that will own and distribute releases.
 *
 * @section FoundationLegalReviewExit Exit Criteria
 *
 * `V1-003` remains `BLOCKED` until all of these are available:
 *
 * - [ ] written confirmation of ownership and authority to license the complete
 *       repository;
 * - [ ] a completed third-party provenance and notice review;
 * - [ ] an approved license text and distribution model;
 * - [ ] approved contribution terms or a documented no-contributions policy;
 * - [ ] consistent license metadata in the repository, documentation, package
 *       contents, and release process;
 * - [ ] a recorded effective version/date and migration plan for already
 *       published material.
 *
 * Replacing the interim notice is a deliberate future change. CI, test, and
 * package success cannot close this legal gate.
 */
